# Which of these countries still had malaria around 1900?
subtitle: "Tap every country that still had malaria around 1900, from Italy to Finland, then hear why it was never really a tropical disease."
tags: ["health","history","geography"]
format: quiz
thumb: "band \"Where did malaria live?\" stamp \"1900\""

## Which of these countries still had malaria around 1900?
sources:
    - id: owid_mal
      title: Malaria was common across half the world – since then it has been eliminated in many regions
      authors: Max Roser (Our World in Data)
      year: 2019
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/malaria-past-prevalence
      finding: around 1900 malaria was prevalent on about 53 % of the world's land, by 2002 on 27 %; it was prevalent in Europe and North America (Lincoln caught it in Illinois, Dürer in the Netherlands, several popes died of it in Italy); 'not a tropical disease, but a disease that was eliminated everywhere except for the tropics'
    - id: hay
      title: 'The global distribution and population at risk of malaria: past, present, and future'
      authors: Hay, Guerra, Tatem, Noor and Snow (The Lancet Infectious Diseases)
      year: 2004
      finding: 'the historical map behind OWID''s chart: malaria''s reach around 1900 and where it was eliminated since'
    - id: cdc
      title: Elimination of malaria in the United States (1947–1951)
      authors: US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention
      year: 1951
      url: https://www.cdc.gov/malaria/history/index.html
      finding: by 1951 malaria was considered eliminated from the United States
    - id: fin
      title: The decline of malaria in Finland – the impact of the vector and social variables
      authors: Lena Hulden and Larry Hulden (Malaria Journal)
      year: 2009
      url: https://malariajournal.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/1475-2875-8-94
      finding: malaria was endemic across Finland in the 18th and 19th centuries; the last indigenous case was in 1954

    cards land cites ["owid_mal","hay"] size 1.12 y 600 arrange rise select "Had malaria" hidden true
        * "USA" icon.of "flag united states" icon.set twemoji in true
        * "Italy" icon.of "flag italy" icon.set twemoji in true
        * "Netherlands" icon.of "flag netherlands" icon.set twemoji in true
        * "Finland" icon.of "flag finland" icon.set twemoji in true
        * "Iceland" icon.of "flag iceland" icon.set twemoji in false
        * "New Zealand" icon.of "flag new zealand" icon.set twemoji in false
    text t_us "until 1951" above land_1 gap 6 #2f6b8f cites ["cdc"] font_size 34 hidden true
    text t_fi "until 1954" below land_4 gap 6 #2f6b8f cites ["fin"] font_size 34 hidden true
    text t_is "never" left land_5 gap 12 #2f6b8f cites ["owid_mal","hay"] font_size 34 hidden true
    text t_nz "never" right land_6 gap 12 #2f6b8f cites ["owid_mal","hay"] font_size 34 hidden true
    sector sec1 #b5482e cites ["owid_mal","hay"] x 270 y 400 radius 182 start 90 end 281 style.fill #d98b72 hidden true
    shape disc1 #5e584e shape circle x 270 y 400 radius 190 hidden true
    text lab1 "1900: half" #b5482e x 270 y 180 font_size 52 hidden true
    sector sec2 #b5482e cites ["owid_mal","hay"] x 730 y 400 radius 182 start 90 end 187 style.fill #d98b72 hidden true
    shape disc2 #5e584e shape circle x 730 y 400 radius 190 hidden true
    text lab2 "2002: a quarter" #b5482e x 730 y 180 font_size 52 hidden true
    text worldland "of the world's land" #5e584e x 500 y 620 font_size 36 hidden true
    text tropics "left over in the tropics" #b5482e cites ["owid_mal"] x 500 y 620 font_size 50 hidden true

    card title "Malaria in 1900: where?"

A: Around 1900, which of these countries still had malaria? Six candidates, from Finland to New Zealand.
    draw land

B: Malaria? That's a tropical disease. I'd say none of them. Italy, at a push.
    highlight land_2

    ask "Tap every country that had it, then Done." on land store m after keep feedback.style warm feedback.perfect "A medical historian in disguise." feedback.good "Most people stop at Italy." feedback.poor "The map surprises almost everyone." feedback.none "Malaria fooled you, as it fooled Europe."
        right: All correct: four of the six had malaria.
        wrong: Some missed. Malaria reached further north than most people think.

A: Italy, yes: several popes died of it. But the Netherlands too, where the painter Albrecht Dürer caught it.
    highlight land_2 land_3

    draw t_us

A: And the United States. Abraham Lincoln caught it in Illinois, and it lingered in the US until 1951.
    highlight land_1

B: Fine. But not Finland. Finland has snow, not mosquitoes.
    highlight land_4

    draw t_fi

A: Finland had both. Malaria was common there, and its last local case was in 1954.
    highlight land_4

    draw t_is t_nz

A: Only Iceland and New Zealand were spared. It never took hold on either.
    highlight land_5 land_6

    erase land t_us t_fi t_is t_nz

A: So around 1900, malaria reached about half of all the land on Earth.
    draw sec1 disc1 lab1 worldland

A: Then drained marshes, better houses and DDT pushed it back. By 2002, a quarter.
    draw sec2 disc2 lab2

    erase worldland

B: So it isn't a tropical disease  (@draw tropics@)at all. It's the disease that's left over in the tropics.

    erase tropics

A: Exactly. Beaten everywhere else, which means it can be beaten.
    text beaten "beaten everywhere else" #2f6b8f cites ["owid_mal"] x 500 y 620 font_size 50

